# SKILL 1 — CHANGE IMPACT & REGRESSION GUARDIAN

## PURPOSE

This skill governs every non-trivial change in an existing codebase.

The primary objective is NOT merely to implement the requested feature.
The objective is to implement it while preserving:

- existing architecture;
- existing business logic;
- existing security guarantees;
- existing cryptographic mechanisms;
- existing API contracts;
- existing user flows;
- existing UI/UX behavior;
- existing performance characteristics;
- existing reliability;
- existing data compatibility;
- existing integrations.

A feature is NOT considered complete merely because new code works.

The agent MUST prove that existing functionality still works correctly after the change.

---

## ABSOLUTE RULE

Never claim that a feature is:

- done;
- complete;
- fully implemented;
- fully integrated;
- all chats are encrypted;
- all flows are working;
- regression-free;

unless the corresponding implementation AND verification have actually been performed.

If verification was not performed, explicitly state:

NOT VERIFIED.

Never infer successful verification from code inspection alone.

---

# 1. BEFORE CHANGING CODE

Before implementation, inspect the existing repository.

Identify:

1. Existing implementations of the requested functionality.
2. Existing abstractions/services/components that already solve part of the problem.
3. Existing crypto mechanisms.
4. Existing data flow.
5. Existing request/response flow.
6. Existing event flow.
7. Existing UI flow.
8. Existing tests.
9. Existing E2E/Playwright tests.
10. Existing performance-sensitive paths.
11. Existing public/internal APIs.
12. Existing persistence/storage mechanisms.
13. Existing error handling.
14. Existing authorization/authentication.
15. Existing observability/logging.

Do NOT immediately create a new implementation.

First determine:

"How does the application currently solve this?"

Prefer extending existing mechanisms over creating parallel implementations.

---

# 2. CHANGE IMPACT MAP

Before coding, create an internal impact map.

At minimum identify:

Requirement
→ entry points
→ domain/service layer
→ security/crypto layer
→ persistence
→ messaging/events
→ transport/network
→ frontend state
→ UI
→ tests
→ E2E
→ performance-sensitive paths.

Identify both:

DIRECT IMPACT
and
INDIRECT IMPACT.

Examples of indirect impact:

- changing message encryption can affect file messages;
- changing authentication can affect WebSocket connections;
- changing call initialization can affect WebRTC;
- changing message DTOs can affect old clients;
- changing chat state can affect unread counters;
- changing crypto initialization can affect reconnect;
- changing database queries can affect pagination performance.

Do not assume that only the edited files are affected.

---

# 3. EXISTING MECHANISM REUSE

Before introducing a new implementation, search the entire repository for an existing mechanism with the same responsibility.

Examples:

- encryption;
- key acquisition;
- key rotation;
- message serialization;
- file upload;
- media upload;
- WebRTC initialization;
- authentication;
- authorization;
- event publishing;
- notification;
- retry;
- caching;
- persistence;
- validation.

If an existing mechanism exists:

1. Reuse it.
2. Extend it if necessary.
3. Refactor it only when justified.
4. Do NOT create a second simplified implementation.

A new parallel implementation requires explicit justification.

If the existing implementation is unsuitable, explain why before replacing or bypassing it.

---

# 4. REGRESSION CHECK — FUNCTIONALITY

After implementation, verify all affected existing flows.

Do not test only the new feature.

For every affected mechanism verify:

- normal operation;
- existing operation;
- error handling;
- retry behavior;
- reconnect behavior;
- concurrent behavior;
- empty/null cases;
- permission failures;
- expired credentials;
- partially initialized state;
- application restart;
- relevant edge cases.

The question is:

"Does the old functionality still behave exactly as intended?"

Not merely:

"Does the new functionality work?"

---

# 5. REGRESSION CHECK — UI

Any frontend change MUST include UI regression verification.

Verify:

- existing layout;
- spacing;
- sizing;
- alignment;
- typography;
- icons;
- buttons;
- menus;
- dialogs;
- responsive behavior;
- scrolling;
- loading states;
- empty states;
- error states;
- disabled states;
- hover/focus states;
- message rendering;
- media rendering;
- existing navigation;
- existing interactions.

Do NOT accept a UI regression merely because the feature technically works.

Examples of regression:

- component moved unexpectedly;
- message layout changed;
- controls disappeared;
- existing buttons changed size;
- mobile layout broke;
- scroll behavior changed;
- media preview became distorted;
- existing component was replaced with a visually inferior implementation.

If a screenshot or visual baseline exists, compare against it.

If Playwright is available, use it.

---

# 6. REGRESSION CHECK — PERFORMANCE

For every change touching a performance-sensitive mechanism, verify performance.

Relevant areas include:

- database queries;
- message loading;
- chat history;
- WebSocket;
- WebRTC;
- encryption/decryption;
- file upload/download;
- media processing;
- rendering;
- React state updates;
- Kafka;
- Redis;
- network calls;
- serialization;
- large lists;
- pagination.

Compare behavior BEFORE and AFTER when practical.

Check:

- latency;
- number of network requests;
- number of database queries;
- payload size;
- CPU usage;
- memory usage;
- rendering behavior;
- unnecessary re-renders;
- startup time;
- encryption overhead;
- upload/download throughput.

Do not accept a feature that is functionally correct but introduces an unjustified performance regression.

If exact benchmarking is unavailable, explicitly state that performance was not quantitatively verified.

---

# 7. REGRESSION CHECK — SEMANTIC BEHAVIOR

Preserve the semantics of existing mechanisms.

Do not silently change:

- ordering;
- lifecycle;
- state transitions;
- retry semantics;
- idempotency;
- transactional boundaries;
- consistency guarantees;
- event ordering;
- authorization semantics;
- encryption guarantees;
- message delivery semantics.

If behavior intentionally changes, explicitly identify the old behavior and the new behavior.

---

# 8. REGRESSION CHECK — SECURITY

Never weaken existing security mechanisms for implementation convenience.

Verify:

- authentication;
- authorization;
- encryption;
- key management;
- validation;
- input handling;
- secrets handling;
- access control;
- transport security;
- data-at-rest protection.

Never replace an existing security mechanism with a simplified local implementation merely because it is easier.

---

# 9. DATABASE AND DATA COMPATIBILITY

Before modifying persistence:

Check:

- existing records;
- existing schema;
- migrations;
- nullable fields;
- indexes;
- constraints;
- old data;
- backward compatibility;
- concurrent access;
- transaction boundaries.

Do not assume an empty database.

Existing production-like data must remain valid.

---

# 10. API COMPATIBILITY

Before modifying an API:

Check:

- backend callers;
- frontend callers;
- WebSocket clients;
- event consumers;
- external integrations;
- DTOs;
- serialization;
- error contracts.

Do not silently break existing consumers.

---

# 11. PLAYWRIGHT / E2E

When Playwright or another E2E framework exists:

1. Identify existing affected tests.
2. Run them before modification when possible.
3. Implement the change.
4. Run affected tests again.
5. Add tests for the new behavior.
6. Test important negative cases.
7. Test the complete user flow, not only the changed component.

Never treat:

"Playwright started successfully"

as equivalent to:

"the feature works."

Tests must contain meaningful assertions.

---

# 12. BASELINE

For important changes establish a baseline before modifying code.

Record where practical:

- existing tests;
- build status;
- E2E status;
- key UI flows;
- performance-sensitive behavior;
- important API behavior.

After implementation compare against the baseline.

Any unexpected regression must be investigated.

---

# 13. FINAL DIFF REVIEW

Before declaring completion:

Inspect the final diff.

Look specifically for:

- accidental changes;
- duplicated implementations;
- dead code;
- debug code;
- temporary workarounds;
- unrelated refactors;
- changed UI behavior;
- bypassed abstractions;
- security shortcuts;
- unnecessary dependencies;
- altered configuration;
- changed error handling.

If unrelated changes appeared, investigate them.

---

# 14. DEFINITION OF DONE

A task can be marked DONE only when:

[ ] requested functionality implemented
[ ] existing mechanisms reused where appropriate
[ ] impact analysis completed
[ ] affected existing functionality tested
[ ] UI regression checked
[ ] performance impact checked where relevant
[ ] security behavior checked
[ ] API compatibility checked
[ ] data compatibility checked
[ ] Playwright/E2E checked where applicable
[ ] negative scenarios checked
[ ] final diff reviewed
[ ] no known regression remains

If any applicable item is unchecked:

STATUS = NOT DONE.

